3477869928e-06,-2.706014311115723e-05,-0.00015293381875380874,-4.8874615458771586e-05,-0.0259858425706625,-0.00018606838420964777,-0.0005977750988677144,-8.546940807718784e-05,-2.264974000354414e-06,0.0,-4.172316494077677e-06,-2.50339189733495e-06,-7.271502545336261e-05,-2.622600959512056e-06,0.0,0.0,-0.0010590903693810105,0.0,-4.410734163684538e-06,-0.19280584156513214,-0.0008175606490112841,-1.006883144378662,-0.7961199283599854,-0.5174484848976135,-3.6545262336730957,-1.828993320465088,-0.6490972638130188,-7.986990567587782e-06,-0.013853820040822029,-4.887569048150908e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,22043,429,279,4843,4647,315,264,52484,8500,374,400,16,15,20,18,3,323,279,42541,4647,374,57960,37018,90,16,18,15170,24,31716,11,1477,279,897,315,279,30387,4647,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nGiven that the third term of a geometric sequence is $1053$ and the ninth term is $\\frac{13}{9}$, find the value of the seventh term.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"94113b47ca0abcd5016994569822e6732ba6e7bdd7d63787567bf4094410121d"},"seconds":23.91658639907837,"seed":693605518311093461,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the seventh term of the geometric sequence, we start by recalling the general form of the \\(n\\)-th term of a geometric sequence:\n\\[\na_n = ar^{n-1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a\\) is the first term and \\(r\\) is the common ratio.\n\nGiven:\n- The third term \\(a_3 = 1053\\)\n- The ninth term \\(a_9 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nUsing the general form, we can write:\n\\[\na_3 = ar^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na_9 = ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9}\n\\]\n\nWe now have two equations:\n1. \\(ar^2 = 1053\\)\n2. \\(ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nTo find \\(r\\), we divide the second equation by the first equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{ar^8}{ar^2} = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053}\n\\]\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053} = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053\n\\]\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify \\(\\frac{13}{9477}\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117}\n\\]\n\nSince 13 is a prime number and does not divide 81 or 117, we keep it as is. Now, we take the sixth root of both sides to solve for \\(r\\):\n\\[\nr^6 = \\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]\n\\[\nr = \\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the seventh term \\(a_7\\):\n\\[\na_7 = ar^6\n\\]\n\nWe already know \\(ar^2 = 1053\\), so:\n\\[\na = \\frac{1053}{r^2}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(a\\) into the expression for \\(a_7\\):\n\\[\na_7 = \\left(\\frac{1053}{r^2}\\right) r^6 = 1053 \\cdot r^4\n\\]\n\nWe need \\(r^4\\):\n\\[\nr^6 = \\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{6}} \\implies r^4 = \\left(\\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{6}}\\right)^4 = \\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\na_7 = 1053 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}} = \\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}} = \\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find:\n\\[\na_7 = 1053 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{13}{9477}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}} = \\boxed{49}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{49}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:753"}
